The informative specimen describes the rooms, window, lamp and thermostat in the original artwork. It is not a screenshot and does not show a current device state. The homepage uses the same artwork as decoration, with empty alt text. The artwork’s source and rights are in `docs/data/illustration-assets.json`.

## Icons

Use Home Assistant’s existing icons. Keep an icon’s meaning the same everywhere. Put a device icon beside its name and state, because a coloured bulb alone does not say which light it is or whether it is available.

Add a visible text label to any unfamiliar action. Give an icon-only control an accessible name and a large enough target. A tooltip adds to that name. It does not replace it.

Do not recolour every icon to the selected palette. Keep warning and error colours, manufacturer artwork that identifies a device, and native state colours that carry meaning.

### Choose an icon

- For a known device or entity, keep its native domain icon and any state variant that means something.
- For a maintained shell action, use one consistent vector symbol. Prefer an existing Home Assistant symbol or a documented component symbol.
- Do not mix emoji, font glyphs, unrelated pictogram sets and hand-drawn icons in one set of controls.
- Treat the visible icon and its target separately. A small, crisp symbol can sit inside a 44px control. Keep its viewBox and aspect ratio, and align neighbouring symbols without distorting them.
- Use `currentColor` where the source allows it. Hide a decorative SVG from assistive technology when the control already has a name.

A replacement icon needs a record of its source, licence, purpose, original geometry and affected components. Test it at its real size in light and dark mode, in forced colours and at high zoom. Do not change manufacturer artwork, supplied civica marks or an informative device state just to make every icon one colour. The [visual review](../community/visual-audit.html) separates real source defects from style preferences.

The maintained lighting scene controls use native Home Assistant Material Design icons at 24px: `mdi:string-lights`, `mdi:soccer`, `mdi:rugby`, `mdi:halloween` and `mdi:alarm-light-outline`. The visible scene name carries the meaning. The small colour strip shows a light recipe, not a theme state. The existing scene illustration files stay as they are. Do not flatten them to white to make an icon set.

### Action-link arrows

The action-link arrow and the search magnifying glass belong to those components. Use the symbol only when you adopt the component’s purpose and interaction. Do not replace device symbols with arrows to copy a homepage. Government crowns, logos and other identity assets are not used in civica.

## Images

Use a room or device image when it helps someone identify the thing. Use a live camera image only in a security or monitoring context, with a visible location and a sign of how fresh it is. Do not add stock photography to fill space.

Give an informative image alternative text. Give a decorative image an empty alternative. Do not repeat the words around it. Keep text, such as device state or instructions, as real text and not inside a bitmap.

## Household identity images

A household or business name can stand alone. If someone chooses a crest or logo, keep its proportions and colours, and keep the name readable without the image. Follow [Personalise your home](../get-started/personalisation.html). Supplied civica artwork belongs to [Project identity](brand.html). Its asset checks do not show that a personalised interface is accessible.
